Windows 11 x64 platform/UA identity

When the shared Windows-identity gate (kHeliumNoise AND
kHeliumNoiseWindowsIdentity AND Linux, via
blink::helium_noise::IsWindowsIdentityEnabled()) is on, every
platform/UA surface presents one coherent Windows 11 x86_64
stock-Chromium identity instead of the honest Linux one:

- navigator.userAgent, navigator.appVersion (derived from the UA
  string), and the User-Agent request header report the Windows 11
  x64 reduced user agent, via the single browser-process seam in
  components/embedder_support/user_agent_utils.cc that produces the
  UA string and UserAgentMetadata for windows and workers alike
  (workers inherit both from the renderer/browser metadata).
- navigator.userAgentData and the Sec-CH-UA-* request headers report
  platform "Windows", the pinned Windows 11 platform version,
  architecture "x86", bitness "64", mobile false, wow64 false, at
  every return path of GetUserAgentMetadata (low-entropy,
  reduced-UA-derived, and full high-entropy). Brand version lists
  keep their stock Chromium values: they are browser-version data,
  not OS data.
- navigator.platform reports Win32 in windows and workers through
  the shared NavigatorBase::platform(); the DevTools platform
  override keeps its precedence in Navigator::platform(), which
  reads the override first.

The Windows platform version is pinned to 19.0.0, derived from the
pinned Chromium 152's own Windows platform-version logic: the
highest known UniversalApiContract version is 19
(kHighestKnownUniversalApiContractVersion in user_agent_utils.cc),
and Chromium falls back to it whenever the WellKnownContracts
registry read fails, so 19.0.0 is the modal value a stock Chromium
152 on Windows 11 reports.

Command-line (--user-agent) and DevTools UA/metadata overrides keep
their existing precedence: the command-line override is consulted
before the identity in GetUserAgent()/GetUserAgentMetadata(), and
DevTools overrides are applied downstream of this seam. Any gate
failure (master off, subfeature off, unsupported platform) returns
exact stock Linux values; there is no partial identity.

arm64 decision: the identity is presented on arm64 Linux builds as
well. The identity is an x86_64 identity by hardcoded design, and an
arm64 Linux desktop cohort is negligibly small next to the Windows
11 x64 anonymity set the identity targets; exposing "arm Linux"
would defeat the blend. IsWindowsIdentityPlatformSupported() in the
shared gate header already returns true on all Linux targets, so no
change is needed there.
